<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A region is split into <m>{3}</m> strips, each <m>{2}\,\text{m}</m> wide. The ordinates (the heights at the edges of the strips), in metres, are <m>y_0, y_1, \ldots, y_{ {3} }</m>.</p><p>Type y0, y1 and so on for the ordinates, for example <c>3*(y0+y1)</c>.</p><p>(a) Write an expression for the area of strip <m>{2}</m>, treated as a trapezium.</p><div><fillin characters="30" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Add the areas of all <m>{3}</m> trapezia. Write the total as one expression in <m>y_0</m> to <m>y_{ {3} }</m>.</p><div><fillin characters="30" name="ans2"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Strip <m>{2}</m> lies between the ordinates <m>y_{ {1} }</m> and <m>y_{ {2} }</m>. A trapezium has area <m>\tfrac{1}{2} \times</m> (sum of the parallel sides) <m>\times</m> width, so its area is <m>{y_{2}+y_{1}}</m>.</p><p>(b) The areas of the <m>{3}</m> strips are <m>{\left[ y_{1}+y_{0} , y_{2}+y_{1} , y_{3}+y_{2} \right]}</m>. Add them. Each inner ordinate is a side of two trapezia, so it is counted twice, while <m>y_0</m> and <m>y_{ {3} }</m> appear once:</p><p><me>A \approx {y_{3}+2 \left(y_{2}+y_{1}\right)+y_{0}}</me></p><p>This is the trapezoidal rule <m>A \approx \tfrac{1}{2}h\left[y_0 + y_n + 2(y_1 + y_2 + \cdots + y_{n-1})\right]</m> with <m>h = {2}</m> and <m>n = {3}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>y_{2}+y_{1}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>y_{3}+2 \left(y_{2}+y_{1}\right)+y_{0}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>